{
  "dom_id": "step-165169",
  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "We always introduce **errors** in our measurements whenever we round or approximate numbers. In this lesson, we will explore how to quantify and describe measurement errors.\n\nSuppose that a particular rope measures precisely $48.9\\,\\textrm{cm}.$ Now, imagine we take a meterstick and record a measurement of $50\\,\\textrm{cm}$ *to the nearest $10$ centimeters*. Let's mark this approximation on our meterstick.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14154806/assets/4e5d5ca05f27aa612b3fdcc9430b495870ddf540630857b6227897d6b20c9e89.png)\n\nThe error of a measurement equals the difference between the rounded estimate and the \"true\" value. So, in this example, the error of our approximation equals\n\n$$\n50\\,\\textrm{cm} - 48.9\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1.1\\textrm{cm}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe often don't know the \"true\" measurement in practical problems. So, the next best thing is to determine a **lower bound** and an **upper bound** for our measurement:\n\n- The *lower* bound of a measurement is its *smallest* possible value.\n\n- The *upper* bound of a measurement is its *largest* possible value.\n\nLet's figure out the lower and upper bounds of our $50\\,\\textrm{cm}$ approximation:\n\n- The *lower bound* is $45 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ because this is the *smallest* value that gives $50 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ when rounded to the nearest $10\\,\\textrm{cm}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14154806/assets/da9cd6208cef81c6fe34fbba9b4271fa3ce1f30df69eb3c631ce6fea4f12e3a3.png)\n\n- The *upper bound* is $55 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ because this is (almost) the *largest* value that gives $50 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ when rounded to the nearest $10\\,\\textrm{cm}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14154806/assets/23a195d88326b587e1864ef04e7b05f74d675f1ddc987baa2cf586af599eb4a9.png)\n\nLet's use the letter $l$ to denote the \"true\" length of the rope. Then, we can combine our lower and upper bound measurements into a single statement, as follows:\n\n$$\n45\\,\\textrm{cm} \\leq l \\lt 55\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used a *strict* \"<\" inequality for the upper bound. We cannot include $55\\textrm{cm}$ in our set of values because this number rounds to $60\\,\\textrm{cm},$ not $50\\,\\textrm{cm}$.\n\nWe can visualize the lower and upper bounds on our diagram as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14154806/assets/1fdacb0787e8316a78da1ad65aa177e9a6f29215fa0d53aef88b0eb33326218d.png)",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
  "html": "<div id=\"step-165169\" class=\"step\" style=\"display: block\" stepid=\"165169\" steptype=\"tutorial\" contentid=\"8751\">\n            <div class=\"stepHeader\">\n                <div class=\"stepName\">\n                    <a id=\"stepAnchor-165169\" class=\"stepAnchor\" name=\"step-1\">Introduction</a>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"helpButton\" stepid=\"165169\">?</div>\n            </div> \n\n            <style type=\"text/css\">\n                            .mjpage .MJX-monospace {\n                            font-family: monospace\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage .MJX-sans-serif {\n                            font-family: sans-serif\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage {\n                            display: inline;\n                            font-style: normal;\n                            font-weight: normal;\n                            line-height: normal;\n                            font-size: 100%;\n                            font-size-adjust: none;\n                            text-indent: 0;\n                            text-align: left;\n                            text-transform: none;\n                            letter-spacing: normal;\n                            word-spacing: normal;\n                            word-wrap: normal;\n                            white-space: nowrap;\n                            float: none;\n                            direction: ltr;\n                            max-width: none;\n                            max-height: none;\n                            min-width: 0;\n                            min-height: 0;\n                            border: 0;\n                            padding: 0;\n                            margin: 0\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage * {\n                            transition: none;\n                            -webkit-transition: none;\n                            -moz-transition: none;\n                            -ms-transition: none;\n                            -o-transition: none\n                            }\n\n                            .mjx-svg-href {\n                            fill: blue;\n                            stroke: blue\n                            }\n\n                            .MathJax_SVG_LineBox {\n                            display: table!important\n                            }\n\n                            .MathJax_SVG_LineBox span {\n                            display: table-cell!important;\n                            width: 10000em!important;\n                            min-width: 0;\n                            max-width: none;\n                            padding: 0;\n                            border: 0;\n                            margin: 0\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage__block {\n                            text-align: center;\n                            margin: 1em 0em;\n                            position: relative;\n                            display: block!important;\n                            text-indent: 0;\n                            max-width: none;\n                            max-height: none;\n                            min-width: 0;\n                            min-height: 0;\n                            width: 100%\n                            }</style><p>We always introduce <strong>errors</strong> in our measurements whenever we round or approximate numbers. In this lesson, we will explore how to quantify and describe measurement errors.</p>\n<p>Suppose that a particular rope measures precisely <span class=\"mjpage\"><svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"8.136ex\" height=\"2.176ex\" style=\"vertical-align: -0.338ex;\" viewBox=\"0 -791.3 3503.2 936.9\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" aria-labelledby=\"MathJax-SVG-1-Title\">\n<title id=\"Math